Fix msm-firmware-loader: create $BASEDIR before mounting a tmpfs on it

Upstream bug in Nura (postmarketOS). msm-firmware-loader.sh mounts a tmpfs at
$BASEDIR without creating the directory first. The service runs very early
(DefaultDependencies=no, Before=systemd-udevd.service), before anything else has
created that path, so the mount fails and the whole loader aborts:

    msm-firmware-loader.sh[405]: mount: /run/msm-firmware-loader: mount point does not exist.

The consequence is that NO vendor firmware partition is ever exposed to the
kernel. On clover that means ath10k has no board calibration data and no MAC,
so Wi-Fi scans but can never associate.

Note the script already does `mkdir -p "$BASEDIR/mnt"` immediately afterwards --
it creates the children but never the parent.

This affects every device that relies on msm-firmware-loader, not just clover.

Apply to /usr/bin/msm-firmware-loader.sh:

--- a/usr/bin/msm-firmware-loader.sh
+++ b/usr/bin/msm-firmware-loader.sh
@@
 # This script is intended to run before udev. This means that writeable fs
 # may not be available yet. Since this script only creates symlinks, it
 # uses tmpfs to work around the early-run limitations as well as to reduce
 # disk wear slightly.
+mkdir -p "$BASEDIR"
 mount -o mode=755,nodev,noexec,nosuid -t tmpfs none "$BASEDIR"

Verified fixed: the unit now reports
    systemd[1]: Finished Load firmware that is located on dedicated partitions of qcom devices.
and /run/msm-firmware-loader/mnt/{vendor,modem,persist,dsp} are populated.

Applied offline with the card's image loop-mounted on a modern kernel; Ubuntu
Touch's 4.4 kernel can only mount that filesystem read-only (orphan_present).
